---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/utils';
import Animations from '../../components/Animations.vue';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { lang: 'en' } },
    { params: { lang: 'es' } },
    { params: { lang: 'pt' } },
  ];
}

const { lang } = Astro.params;
const t = useTranslations(lang);

const content = {
  en: {
    title: "Dear Diary, Why Every Developer Should Learn Git Beyond `git push` 🌸",
    date: "August 31, 2026",
    subtitle: "A developer diary by Jill",
    back: 'Back to Home',
    intro: "When I first started using Git... my workflow looked something like this.",
    introDesc: "git add . → git commit -m \"changes\" → git push",
    introExtra: [
      "That was it.",
      "As long as my code reached GitHub, I thought I was using Git correctly.",
      "Turns out... I was only using a tiny fraction of what Git can actually do."
    ],
    firstAssumption: {
      heading: "My first misconception",
      paragraphs: [
        "For a long time I thought Git was simply a backup. Write code. Commit. Push. Repeat.",
        "But Git isn't Dropbox for developers. Git is a complete version control system designed to help people build software together.",
        "It remembers every decision. Every change. Every experiment. Every mistake. And that's incredibly powerful."
      ]
    },
    gitNotGithub: {
      heading: "Git isn't GitHub",
      paragraphs: [
        "One of the first things that confused me was thinking Git and GitHub were the same thing. They're not.",
        "Git is the version control system. GitHub is a platform that hosts Git repositories.",
        "You can use Git without GitHub. And you can use GitHub because Git exists.",
        "Understanding that distinction makes many Git concepts much easier."
      ]
    },
    everyCommit: {
      heading: "Every commit tells a story",
      paragraphs: [
        "A commit shouldn't just save code. It should explain why something changed."
      ],
      badLabel: "Instead of meaningless commit messages:",
      goodLabel: "Try writing descriptive messages:",
      closing: "Months later... future you will be incredibly grateful."
    },
    branches: {
      heading: "Branches are parallel universes",
      paragraphs: [
        "One concept I really enjoy is branching.",
        "Imagine your project is on a single timeline (main). Now you need a new feature.",
        "Instead of risking the production code... you create a branch. Each branch becomes an isolated workspace where you can experiment safely."
      ]
    },
    conflicts: {
      heading: "Merge conflicts aren't Git being mean",
      paragraphs: [
        "At first... merge conflicts felt terrifying. I thought I had broken the repository.",
        "Usually, Git is simply saying: \"Two people changed the same piece of code. I don't know which version you want.\"",
        "For example, if Developer A and Developer B edit the same tax variable differently, Git can't guess. So it asks you to resolve the conflict."
      ]
    },
    timeTravel: {
      heading: "Git helps you travel through time",
      paragraphs: [
        "One of my favorite Git features is being able to inspect history and compare changes.",
        "Once I understood this... I stopped being afraid of making mistakes. Because most mistakes are recoverable."
      ]
    },
    pullRequests: {
      heading: "Pull Requests are conversations",
      paragraphs: [
        "One thing I didn't appreciate early on is that Pull Requests aren't only about merging code. They're about communication.",
        "A good Pull Request explains what changed, why, and any risks involved. Code reviews often improve software long before users see it."
      ]
    },
    teamwork: {
      heading: "Git makes teamwork possible",
      paragraphs: [
        "Without version control... collaboration becomes chaos.",
        "Git allows multiple developers to work on features, review code, track changes, and recover previous versions. Large software projects simply wouldn't be practical without it."
      ]
    },
    cheatsheet: {
      heading: "My most used Git commands",
      paragraphs: [
        "Over time, these have become part of my daily workflow. Notice that only one of them is git push! The rest help me understand and manage the project."
      ]
    },
    takeaway: {
      heading: "Technical takeaway 🌸",
      paragraphs: [
        "Git isn't just about sending code to GitHub. It's about managing change.",
        "Good Git practices include:"
      ],
      items: [
        "Small, meaningful commits",
        "Clear commit messages",
        "Feature branches",
        "Pull Requests",
        "Code reviews",
        "Keeping main stable"
      ],
      closing: "Learning these habits early makes collaboration much easier."
    },
    personalNote: {
      heading: "Personal note 🌷",
      paragraphs: [
        "One thing I enjoy about software development is realizing that every tool has layers.",
        "At first, Git felt like a list of commands to memorize. Now... it feels more like a timeline. A record of how an idea evolved.",
        "Every commit represents a small decision. Every branch represents an experiment. And every merge tells part of the story of the software. I think that's a beautiful way to look at version control."
      ]
    },
    remember: {
      heading: "Things I Want To Remember 💖",
      items: [
        "Git and GitHub are different things.",
        "Commits should explain why, not just what.",
        "Branches make experimentation safe.",
        "Merge conflicts are conversations, not disasters.",
        "Version control is one of the most important tools a developer can learn."
      ]
    },
    closing: "Next entry: TypeScript Didn't Make Me Faster 🌸",
    closingSign: "It made me more confident. Every abstraction leaks.",
    signature: "— Jill"
  },
  es: {
    title: "Querido Diario, Por Qué Cada Desarrollador Debería Aprender Git Más Allá de git push 🌸",
    date: "31 de Agosto, 2026",
    subtitle: "El diario de desarrollo de Jill",
    back: 'Volver al Inicio',
    intro: "Cuando comencé a usar Git... mi flujo de trabajo se veía más o menos así.",
